<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to the type-versus-value distinction

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.TypesAndValues-->
<docs:TypesAndValues xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to the type-versus-value distinction
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: a type describing possible values versus an instance holding an actual value,
    grounded in org.blockml.bml.core.ValueType and
    org.blockml.bml.documentation.EverythingIsABlock. Body content is authored in a03 from those
    sources.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="core:ValueType" relation="Requires">
      The typed value domain this page's distinction is built on.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:EverythingIsABlock" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — ValueTypes never store data; Properties own all concrete values.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:ValueTypeConfiguration" relation="SeeAlso">
      How a Property's owned type instance, not the ValueType definition, carries configuration.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Types and Values</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <dpage:Section heading="Type versus value">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A type describes the space of possible values something may hold; an instance is
the one concrete value actually chosen from that space. BlockML keeps these two roles
strictly apart everywhere: a type answers what kind of value is possible, and an
instance answers which value was actually chosen.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Types never hold data">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A ValueType only describes the nature of a value; it never stores one itself. Every
concrete value is owned exclusively by the Property that declares that type, so the
type and the data it describes never mix.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="A value or none, never null">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A Property either holds a value from its declared type or holds none at all, so
there is no separate null state to account for beyond the type-versus-value
distinction itself.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="One distinction, many member kinds">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>The same type-versus-value separation underlies every member kind. A Property's
declared type, and any configuration applied to that Property's own type instance,
both still describe nature rather than hold a value themselves — configuration
applies to the instance's type, never to the ValueType definition itself.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with Generic Types">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Generic Types" summary="Learn how a type can leave part of its shape open as a generic parameter.">
                  <target>docs:GenericTypes</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:TypesAndValues>
